{{also|mürk}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/mɝk/|a=GenAm}}
* {{IPA|en|/mɜːk/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-murk.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɜː(ɹ)k|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|merke}}, {{m|enm|mirke}}, from {{inh|en|ang|mirce}}, {{m|ang|myrce|t=dark, gloomy, evil}} and {{der|en|non|myrkr|t=dark, murky}}, both from {{der|en|gem-pro|*merkuz|t=dark}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*mergʷ-|t=to flicker; to darken; to be dark}}. Cognate {{cog|da|mørk|t=dark}}, {{cog|no|mørk|t=dark}}, {{cog|sv|mörk|t=dark}}, {{cog|is|myrkur|t=dark}}, as also {{cog|sq|murg|t=dark}}, {{cog|sla-pro|*morkъ|t=darkness}}, {{cog|lt|márgas|t=multicolored}}, {{cog|grc|ἀμορβός|t=dark}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|en|mirk|q1=archaic|mark|q2=dialectal}}

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}

# [[dark|Dark]], [[murky]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1835|author=w:Joseph Rodman Drake|title=The Culprit Fay
|passage=He cannot see through the mantle '''murk'''.}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en|mirk}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|murken}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|mirke}}, {{m|enm|merke}}, from {{inh|en|ang|mirce}}, {{m|ang|myrce|t=darkness, gloom}} and {{der|en|non|myrkr|t=darkness, gloom}}, both from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*merkwą}}, {{m|gem-pro|*mirkwiz|t=darkness}}, {{der|en|ine-pro|*mergʷ-|t=to flicker; to darken; to be dark}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-}}

# [[darkness|Darkness]], or a dark or gloomy environment.
#: {{syn|en|gloom}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare All's Well|2|1|passage={{...}}in '''murk''' and occidental damp}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Walt Whitman|title=Sequel to Drum-Taps: When Lilacs Last in the Dooryard Bloom’d and other poems|chapter=[[w:When Lilacs Last in the Dooryard Bloom'd|When Lilacs Last in the Dooryard Bloom’d]]|year=1865|passage=O great star disappear’d—O the black '''murk''' that hides the star!}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|murky}}

